  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{\u00a0}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{\u00a0}\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:  \n\n$$\n(x-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{\u00a0}\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{\u00a0}\\,\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\n   \n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{\u00a0}\\,\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{\u00a0}\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
